# Example inbox taxonomy - safe to commit. Copy to config.yaml and edit:
#   cp config.yaml.example config.yaml
#
# `label` is the string the classifier actually sees. Keep it to two or three words that NAME
# the class: GLiNER matches class names, and full sentences collapse the whole inbox onto one
# answer (measured: 7 of 8 emails -> 'skip'). Bare folder names work but confuse neighbours --
# a card charge filed as Income until Expenses became 'purchase receipt'.
#
# `description` is for humans, and is what the openjev-email branch scored as a hypothesis.
#
# There is no spam_signals list here: spam is asked as a two-class question, so P(spam) is a
# real probability. See web/questions.js.
folders:
- name: Receipts
  description: This is a receipt, invoice, or order confirmation for something the recipient bought, with an amount stated.
  label: purchase receipt
- name: Statements
  description: This is a periodic account statement from a bank or credit card.
  label: bank statement
- name: Travel
  description: 'This is about a trip: a flight, hotel, reservation, or ticket.'
  label: trip booking
- name: Newsletters
  description: 'This is a periodical the recipient subscribed to and reads at leisure: a digest or roundup.'
  label: newsletter
- name: Work
  description: This addresses the recipient as an employee, about internal company matters.
  label: internal company mail
operations:
- op: delete
  description: This is a promotional advertisement from a shop, offering a sale, a discount, a deal or reward points.
  label: advertisement
- op: skip
  description: This needs the operator to read it and decide personally, because it concerns something only they can judge.
  label: needs a personal decision
- op: archive
  description: This is ordinary correspondence that has served its purpose and just needs filing away out of the inbox.
  label: routine correspondence
